344:## What I can prove today.
346:### Receipts behind the argument
352:<ClaimBadge kind="observed" /> **The reviewer copy.** The drift-check source records a deployed reviewer missing its mandatory establish-the-diff step while the upstream copy contained it. The incident was filed August 5; the check was committed August 6. Its five-day interval describes the stale deployment. Comparing deployed bytes with the source makes that discrepancy visible, but doesn't recover a per-session loading history or count incorrect reviews.[^drift]
354:<ClaimBadge kind="observed" /> **Sibling instructions.** One harness-specific instruction file carried superseded doctrine for six weeks after its canonical sibling changed. The parity gate committed August 5 checks a declared relationship between those files. It doesn't require every document to be identical. The separate live-document staleness check catches a named obsolete assertion outside that pair; it leaves dated historical records alone.[^parity][^staleness]
356:<ClaimBadge kind="observed" /> **Workflow copies that needed different repairs.** The synchronization source records both missing upstream review checks and local copies with stricter conditional-verdict handling. The August 11 implementation preserves per-project rosters and refuses local-ahead overwrites. That refusal protects the difference from erasure; it doesn't certify the difference as correct or promote it into shared policy.[^workflow]
358:<ClaimBadge kind="measured" /> **Ancestry versus released content.** The retained maintenance record reports negative commit-ancestry and tag-containment checks, followed by direct inspection of the release's file, which found the fix. The repository's squash-merge history explains the discrepancy. This measures presence in the tagged source, not presence in a deployed process, and supplies no outcome comparison.[^ancestry]
360:<ClaimBadge kind="measured" /> **A provenance writer on one path.** The September 16 audit traced automatic BOM emission through composite/bundle materialization and inspected the separate single-artifact deployment route. The latter emitted activity events without invoking the BOM generator. The finding is a path-coverage gap. It doesn't measure the proportion of all deployments missing records, and a working bundle path doesn't close that gap.[^bom-scope]
362:<ClaimBadge kind="measured" /> **Real records, unresolved caller.** The same audit inspected three real context-provenance rows with deployment findings and traced the CLI's call into the writer. It found no durable dispatch log in the inspected canonical checkout that identified which actor produced those rows. That leaves the actor unresolved. It doesn't make the rows fictitious, and it doesn't prove the automated pilot produced them.[^context-records]
364:<ClaimBadge kind="observed" /> The compact chronology below comes from the first commits of the named local mechanisms. These are implementation-history markers, not public-priority claims or dates of complete fleet coverage.
373:<ClaimBadge kind="proposed" /> Those repairs support the managed-variation architecture in this essay. They don't establish a complete identity-to-outcome chain. I still need evidence for when a local improvement should become shared policy, when an automated fast path should return to judgment, and how an independent acceptance decision changes results. The three-project comparison is illustrative. No promotion, demotion or acceptance-by-assertion incident is being claimed from these receipts.
375:<ClaimBadge kind="external" /> The four vendor descriptions and the related architecture research are outside sources. Their product boundaries and contribution claims remain distinct from my implementation records. In particular, no external benchmark result is reported here as a benefit measured in the lab.
